---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Container from '../components/astro/Container.astro';
import NarrativeSection from '../components/astro/NarrativeSection.astro';
import SectionHero from '../components/astro/SectionHero.astro';
import TakeawayCard from '../components/astro/TakeawayCard.astro';
import Callout from '../components/astro/Callout.astro';
import PrincipleList from '../components/astro/PrincipleList.astro';
import DetailExpander from '../components/react/DetailExpander';

const signatureFindings = [
  {
    title: 'Surface compliance can still hide substantive drift',
    body: 'One of the most interesting failures kept citation rules clean while still letting weaker workflow commentary reshape the real determination.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The root cause was upstream plumbing',
    body: 'A key failure traced back to the chunking pipeline rather than the spec documents or the model itself. The diagnosis mattered more than the blame.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Well-maintained documents are still the foundation',
    body: 'When enterprise departments maintain weak or contradictory sources, agents fail in the same ways a human analyst would fail.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Good governance shrinks the agent',
    body: 'As the Context Contract and orchestration layer became clearer, the domain agents got smaller, narrower, and easier to reason about.',
  },
];

const evaluationQuestions = [
  {
    title: 'Did the system retrieve the right sources?',
    body: 'Evidence selection is the first quality gate. A correct answer from the wrong source still counts as a governance problem.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Did the system preserve enterprise constraints?',
    body: 'Policy precedence, permissions, and escalation semantics matter as much as answer fluency.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Did the system overfit to local instructions?',
    body: 'A good run should honor both enterprise-wide rules and task-specific context without collapsing one into the other.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Did more context actually help?',
    body: 'The notes make clear that context volume is not a monotonic win. The goal is scoped context, not maximal context.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Did examples outperform pure rules?',
    body: 'Several notes point toward behavioral examples and fixtures being stronger adherence signals than abstract prose constraints alone.',
  },
];

const baselineConditions = [
  {
    index: '01',
    title: 'Naive RAG',
    body: 'Single index, top-k retrieval, weak authority discipline. Useful as a foil because it surfaces how much governance is being delegated to similarity search.',
  },
  {
    index: '02',
    title: 'Long-context dump',
    body: 'Shows that feeding everything to the model is not the same as making the right evidence easy to use. Auditing also becomes much weaker.',
  },
  {
    index: '03',
    title: 'Governed pipeline',
    body: 'Preserves status semantics, authority tiers, and explicit escalation behavior. The point is not style; it is inspectability.',
  },
];

const honestClaims = [
  'This work shows a concrete, governed architecture for one enterprise workflow; it does not claim statistical generality.',
  'The demo replay is captured, not live. That makes the decision trail easier to study and keeps the presentation focused.',
  'The value proposition is the architecture and evaluation discipline, not a claim to have built a universal enterprise AI platform.',
];

const futureWork = [
  {
    title: 'Second workflow corpus',
    body: 'Re-run the same spec stack against a different enterprise workflow to stress-test what generalizes and what is scenario-specific.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Evaluation overlays in the replay',
    body: 'Show fixture outcomes, spec excerpts, and failure annotations directly against each replay step.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Adversarial fixture generation',
    body: 'Use an LLM red-team process to propose new scenario failures beyond the hand-authored suite.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Findings">
  <section class="border-b border-ink-100">
    <Container width="wide" class="py-16 md:py-24">
      <SectionHero
        as="h1"
        eyebrow="Part 07 · Findings"
        title="What the work demonstrated, what surprised it, and what it should not overclaim."
        lede="The most useful learnings were not generic benchmark wins. They were design discoveries: where governance had to become structural, where evaluation had to become behavioral, and where the real failure causes actually lived."
      />
    </Container>
  </section>

  <NarrativeSection id="signature-findings" tone="default" width="wide">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Signature findings</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">
        Four conclusions worth carrying forward
      </h2>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4">
      {
        signatureFindings.map((finding) => (
          <TakeawayCard title={finding.title}>{finding.body}</TakeawayCard>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <Callout kind="rationale" title="The subtle failure mode that matters most">
      The most interesting failure was not a blatant rules violation. It was a case of surface
      compliance with substantive drift. The agent stayed tidy at the citation layer while letting
      lower-authority context reshape the actual determination. That is exactly the class of failure
      that shape checks alone would miss.
    </Callout>
  </NarrativeSection>

  <NarrativeSection id="evaluation" tone="muted" width="wide">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Evaluation logic</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">
        The project evaluated behavior, not just final answers
      </h2>
      <p class="lede mt-4">
        The working notes repeatedly return to the same theme: correctness without traceability is
        not enough, and fluency without escalation discipline is worse than useless in an enterprise
        setting.
      </p>
    </header>

    <PrincipleList items={evaluationQuestions} />

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
      {
        baselineConditions.map((condition) => (
          <TakeawayCard index={condition.index} title={condition.title}>
            {condition.body}
          </TakeawayCard>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <DetailExpander
      summary="Why adversarial fixtures became the fastest feedback loop"
      kind="spec"
      client:visible
    >
      The fixtures forced vague prose to become operational. If a spec said an agent must preserve a
      status boundary, there needed to be a scenario that proved whether the system actually did.
      That made fixture writing a design activity, not just a testing afterthought.
    </DetailExpander>
  </NarrativeSection>

  <NarrativeSection id="reflections" tone="default" width="wide">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Reflections</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">
        What the build clarified
      </h2>
    </header>
    <div class="prose-narrative mt-6 max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        The notes reveal a shift in emphasis over time. Early on, the instinct was to keep exploring
        ever-larger context strategies and more ambitious automation. By the end, the clearer
        position was almost the opposite: narrow the agents, tighten the document jurisdictions, and
        make the supervisor more explicit.
      </p>
      <p>
        Another useful clarification was presentational. The right story is not that a
        vendor-onboarding demo was built. The right story is that a governed method for enterprise
        AI was built and then demonstrated through vendor onboarding. That distinction keeps the
        scenario in its proper place.
      </p>
    </div>
  </NarrativeSection>

  <NarrativeSection id="scope" tone="muted" width="wide">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Scope discipline</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">
        What the presentation should claim honestly
      </h2>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-3">
      {
        honestClaims.map((claim) => (
          <article class="panel p-5">
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">{claim}</p>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </NarrativeSection>

  <NarrativeSection id="future-work" tone="default" width="wide">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Future work</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-display-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">
        Where this could grow without losing focus
      </h2>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
      {futureWork.map((item) => <TakeawayCard title={item.title}>{item.body}</TakeawayCard>)}
    </div>
  </NarrativeSection>
</BaseLayout>
